## Lesson Y1 (G34): AS WHITE, 6 COLUMNS x 6 ROWS = 36 < 38. GET A STONE BEYOND THE WALL
- vs B G7 + G3 + D7 (black on top AND right), W C4/E5 then E3 (bottom) made a box: black
  answered C6 with C7, E6 with E7, F6 with G6, F4 with G4 -> black's L-wall row 7 + col G.
  My max = A-F x rows 1-6 = 36, minus edge losses = 30. Count at move ~12, not 20.
- After B D7: play C7/C8 (3-3 under D7, top-left corner) or H4/H5 (right side between
  G3 and G7) — a stone that can LIVE on black's side — before pushing along row 6.
- Late kill tries (G8 cut at move 37) fail once the target group has 6+ libs and an eye.

## Lesson V1 (G33): A SIDE GROUP LINKED TO HOME BY ONE EMPTY POINT IS A CUT WAITING
- After J6 my H5-H6-J6-J7 group had libs H4/J5 only; H4 was ALSO its link to G4. W H4 =
  cut + atari, 4 stones dead. Before every move, run libs.py and for each side group ask
  "which empty point connects it home?" — if the opponent can play there, play it first.
- vs an invasion under my framework (W E3 under C4/E5/G3): block on the side where it can
  reach the EDGE/my weakest stone (C3 under C4), not the cap/connection (E4, D4). The
  invader connected out along the left edge (B2, B4, B5) and my B3 hane became its eye.
- Opening note: C4 + G3 + E5 vs W G7 + C7 (parallel split) gave a 5-row framework that
  was too thin to stop E3. After W G5, maybe F3/E3 myself (secure the bottom) instead of E5.

## Lesson U1 (G32): AS WHITE vs B G7 + G4 (two stones on one side), DON'T PLAY E5
- E5 between them works vs G7+G3 (G26) but vs G4 (4th line) Black attached E6 and my
  answers (D6, F5, D5, F4) were all centre stones. Then B E3 slid under the wall.
- Better 2nd move: take a LOW base on the open side, e.g. C6/C7 (2nd left corner) or E3
  (under G4, bottom is open below a 4th-line stone). White needs 38 = left 4 columns +
  2 more points, so the bottom row 1-3 must be mine.
- When Black invades under my wall (E3), answer from the EDGE side with a stone that has
  3+ libs (E2/D2 contact under, not F4 from above). A 2-lib block (D3) gets haned (D2).

## Lesson L1 (G31): AS BLACK, DON'T PLAY THE CENTRE WHEN FOUR CORNER STONES ARE DIAGONAL
- B C4, W G3, B G6, W C7: I played E5 -> W D2 undercut C4, W H5 invaded the right side,
  W B6/B5/B4 slid down the left, W F9 the top. My answers came from ABOVE (E3/D3, G5, C5)
  each time, so White got every edge and I got a ~32-point centre block.
- Better 3rd move: an edge-side move that makes a base and attacks, e.g. E3 (pincer G3 and
  protect the C4 corner's bottom) or E7 (pincer C7, protect G6's top). Make my two
  stones into edge territory before the centre.
- vs an invasion/undercut, block from the EDGE side (H4 under W H5, cutting it from G3;
  C2/C3 under W D2), not from the centre side.
- Count honestly: a boundary not yet closed counts for nobody. At move 19 I counted 41,
  real was ~35.

## Lesson B1 (G29): A WALL WITHOUT A BASE IS NOT A GROUP — COUNT EYES, NOT LIBERTIES
- My 2nd stone G4 (4th line) near W G7 got undercut (W F3) and capped (E5). Every move I
  added (G3, F5, H5, H4) made a longer wall between W's top and W's bottom stones.
- Before ANY cut/attack, for each of my groups list its eye points. If fewer than 2 sure
  eyes and no connection: play the base move FIRST (2nd line under the wall: G2/H2, or
  extend along the edge where there's room: H6). W G2 then H2 took my base in 2 moves.
- Second move vs W corner stone: 3rd line (G3/C7) so the stone has its own base.

## White vs Black high framework (G7 3-3 + C7 + E5) that WON (G30, W+4 vs 10k)
W C4, G4, E4 (row-4 wall, one-space jumps). Answer the attach (G5) solidly (H4), take the
left side with C6 under C7 + C5. Black's wedges into the wall (F4, D4, D3) die if I answer
from UNDER (F3, E2, C3, B2) and capture whatever is in atari first (F5, D1, F1).
In the bottom-right corner, black J4+H3 lives because J5 connects (my J5 = self-atari).

## White vs tengen + B F7 that WON (G38, W+4 vs 16k)
W C4, W G3 (both low on the open bottom). B E3 wedge -> W D3, B D2 hane -> W C2, connect
C3 when ataried; then F3 attach (wedge down to 2 libs) and E2 (hits wedge + D2 stones).
Left: B5 hane, B3 atari on the B4 cut, A4 capture, A5. Count hit 35 at move 26 -> take the
right side up the edge (H5, H4, H6, G5, J7, J6) = 39. Kill bottom invasions with J2/H1.

## White vs tengen that WON (G28, W+6 vs 12k) — REUSE vs E5 + a black corner stone
W C4, B G3, W G7 (corner diagonal from C4, away from G3), B C7, W E7, B D6, W D4, B E4,
W E3 (hane under), B F3, W D3, B E2, W D2, then E6 (head, attached to E7), G5, F5 cut.
Each of my groups had its own corner + edge, so no sandwich. Count at move ~42 = 40.

## Lesson C1 (G27): KEEP THE FIRST CORNER — A 2-LIB CORNER STONE IS WORTH ~8 POINTS
- B C4, W D5 (shoulder), B F4, W D4, B D3, W C5: now C4 has 2 libs (B4, C3). C3 (or B4)
  at once keeps A1-C3 + D3 base (~8 pts). I played G6 and lost C4 + corner -> lost by 2.
- Black's winning shape vs this: bottom rows 1-3 full width + right side cols G-J. That's
  27 + 15 + a few = 45+. Losing either bottom corner = loss. Count at move ~20.

## White vs Black 3-3 + same-side stone that WON (G26, W+4 vs 14k)
(G34 caveat: if B answers E5 with D7 instead of E4, don't push under; see lesson Y1.)
B G7, W C4, B G3, W E5!, B E4 attach -> W D4 (hane toward my corner), B D3 -> W C3,
B E2, W F5 (hane at the head), B F6 -> W E6, B E7 -> W D7, B D8 -> W C7 (connect),
B C8 -> W B8, then G6 atari (B F7), G5 connect, F4 atari (B E3), H6, G4, J6, A8, H3/H4, J4.
Area: cols A-D + center row 4-6 + right H/J rows 3-9 = 39. Thin (needed 38): count at move
~35 and play every 1-point dame (J7/J8/J9) — they decided the margin.
When B invades a 10-point area of mine late: connect my cut points first (D5/D6), then take
the eye points from the outside (B6, B4); a 4-stone group with 2 libs and no eye is dead.

## Lesson K2 (G25): WHEN BEHIND, HUNT FOR A GROUP WITH NO OUTSIDE LIBERTIES
- A big edge group whose liberties are ALL inside its own eye space (6-7 points in a corner)
  is killable. Steps that worked: shrink it from the side first (B1 atari on the C1 hane,
  D1 capture), then 2-2 point inside (H2); when it blocks at 2-1 (H1), atari from the 3-1
  (G1). Answer the double atari by capturing (J1). Then fill my own eye only if both
  sides keep shared libs (H1), and wait for the opponent to self-atari.
- Moves inside the opponent's area cost nothing in area scoring: always try before passing.
- SEKI: if my eyeless group and their groups share all the libs and any approach move
  would be self-atari, stop. Don't fill a shared liberty (A9/D7 in G25 would have died).
- Opening note: vs W C6 + E7 (top) and a G3 invasion under my F4, I blocked E3 and let W
  crawl right to J3 -> W got ~16 pts on rows 1-3. Block toward the side with MORE room.

## Lesson S1 (G24): IN A FIGHT INSIDE MY AREA, CAPTURE THE GROUP THAT IS IN ATARI. CHECK SNAPBACK.
- Position (move 54): my 12-stone left group had only 2 libs/eye points (D2 eye + E1). Black
  F1-F2-G1 was IN ATARI (only lib E1). I instead captured one stone J2 with J1 -> my
  G2-H2-H1-J1 group's ONLY liberty was the captured point J2 -> Black played J2 = SNAPBACK
  (4 stones). Then Black's F-group had libs again and E1 for Black would leave my big group
  one eye -> dead -> resign. E1 (capturing 3) would have settled EVERYTHING and won.
- RULE: before any capture, run libs.py and ask (1) is any enemy group in atari that touches
  my weakest group? capture THAT one first; (2) after my capturing move, how many libs does
  my capturing group have? If its only lib is the point just emptied -> snapback, don't.
- RULE: a big group with eye space of only 2 points (D2 + E1, where E1 touches an enemy
  stone) has ONE real eye. Whenever enemy stones are next to my eye space, count real eyes.
- Earlier cause: my bottom (D1-F2) was left open with my left group at 3 libs (D1/D2/D3);
  Black's D2/F2 throw-ins started the fight. When ahead, close the 2nd line (D2 or E2)
  before the last territory moves.
- Good in G24 (from the move-16 position): G2 (hane under black G3), H5 (connect), H2,
  D5 (split), F5 (take a lib + connect), J3 (atari, forced E6), H7/G8/F8/H8/G9 = whole
  top-right; C3/C2/C1 solid vs black's B4-B3-B2-B1 crawl.
  Climb one rung per win; drop one rung after two straight losses; a draw = stay.

## Lesson Z1 (G23): MY "TERRITORY" GROUP CAN DIE — RUN libs.py ON MY OWN BIG GROUP EVERY MOVE
- A group in the center whose area is open on the far side (F-J rows 4-9 with no stone on
  the edge) is NOT territory: W invaded (G7) and lived in the corner, W lived below (F3/G3),
  and the rest of the boundary closed. Final: 15 stones, libs 3, no eye space -> resign.
- From move ~30 I played endgame (F7, F9, E9, D2, C2) without checking that group. Before
  any endgame move: "does each of my groups have 2 eyes or a sure connection?" If a group
  touches no edge and is surrounded by 3+ enemy groups, make eyes NOW (here: J4/J5 + H3-J3
  space, or J6/H7 early to stop W's top-right group from taking the right edge).
- vs W E4 (two columns from my C4): E6 then the E5 cut made 2 weak black groups. Untested
  better ideas: answer W D5 by solidly connecting my side (D6 or C5 at once) instead of
  cutting, or answer W E4 with a low pincer/approach (E3/F3 side) so W can't wall off.

## Lesson P1 (G22): AS WHITE vs TENGEN, C4+G4 FAILS IF BLACK WEDGES E3 AND WALLS E2-E6
- Two bottom corners split by a black wall = ~32 points = loss. When B also has C6 (above
  my C4), the left corner can't grow upward. Prefer C4 + G6 (G12 draw) or C4 + G7: groups
  on DIFFERENT sides, so no single black wall can sandwich both.
- After the wedge, answer at once on the bottom edge (D2/F2) so the wall can't reach row 2;
  B E2 then D2/G2 hanes cut both my corners down.
- Never extend a group into a long wall between black stones (D6/D5) while its eye space is
  still open. Make eye shape first: in the corner, the 1-2 point (B1/B2) is vital. Black B1
  gave A1/B1/B2 = one eye.
- Don't start a second weak group (E7 top invasion) while my bottom groups are unsettled.

## Lesson M1 (G21): AS BLACK vs W F4 (MIRROR LOW REPLY) — DON'T BUILD A CENTER
- B C4, W F4, B F6?, W D6 (crosscut shape), B E5, W E6, B F5 -> I owned the center, W got
  B6/D6/E6 + F7/G6/H6 = the whole top (rows 6-9) AND then the left corner. 34 pts = lost.
- W ignored my F4 atari (G4) to play G6 and seal the top: 1 stone for 15+ pts. Before an
  atari ask "what does W get if it tenukis?" — the sealing move was worth more than F4.
- Untested better ideas vs W F4: take the top first with a 3rd/4th-line stone (D6/E6 or
  G6/F7 side) so my area is LEFT + TOP and F4 stays a weak stone in my sphere; or attach
  under F4 (F3/G3) to deny it a base. Whatever I play, count at move ~15: need 45.
- Hane stone at 2 libs (B3 after W A3): connect on the EDGE side (B2) when my inside is
  already strong; C3 handed W the corner (6 pts).

## Lesson R1 (G20): EVERY GROUP NEEDS "EYES OR A CONNECTION" — CHECK EACH MOVE
- When B attaches under my 4th-line stone (B G3 under W G4) and slides (G2, F2, E2, D1),
  black gets a LIVING group in my bottom. My stones above it (G4/H4/J4/J5) then have NO eye
  space and must stay CONNECTED to my main group. The link point was F4 (between F3 and G4).
  When B approached it (F6 then F5), F4 was urgent; I played G5 and D5 (territory) and B F4
  cut -> 7 stones died, game lost. Rule: for each of my groups ask "two eyes, or connected to
  a group with two eyes?" If neither, fix that before any territory move.
- Better answer to the attach-under G3: hane on the side where my OTHER stones are AND
  plan the wall's connection (F3 then F4 solid), or hane H3 and let B take the F-side while my
  wall connects. Never let my wall sit on top of a living enemy group without a link.

## White vs tengen that WON BIG (G18, W+38 vs 14k)
W C4, then G4 (other low side). When B wedges between (E3), attach on the wedge from the
side with more room (F3), connect cut points at once (G3), and make each side live on its own
(D3/D4 left, G2/H4/J4 right). Then the black wall between my groups is SANDWICHED: attack it
from above with moves attached to my own wall (D6, E7 = miai D7/E6, F6 cut, G7 seal). A wall
whose only eye space is the 1st line under it is dead. Count its eye space before attacking.
CAVEAT (G22): vs 12k the same C4/G4 line lost: Black's wall lived (connected to G6/C6
on top), and my two corners were too small. Only works if the wall can actually be killed.

## Lesson K1 (G17): AS BLACK, WHEN WHITE GOES LOW (rows 1-3), TAKE THE TOP NEXT
- W strip rows 1-3 = 27+7 = 34 -> I win if I just own rows 4-9 with no W life there. But rows
  4-9 is 6 rows deep: a W invasion at C6/F7 lives unless I have a stone up there. After my
  row-4 wall is connected (C4-D4 + F4), the next move is a top stone like D7/E7 (3rd line from
  the top), NOT G4/H4 etc. Row-4 holes can wait until W actually pushes.
- When an invader arrives (W C6), attack from the side that keeps it away from the EDGE and
  from its friends (B5/B4 link to C3). An invader that reaches the edge (A6/B7/A8) gets eyes.
- In a race between two weak groups, find the opponent group with the FEWEST libs that also
  separates mine (G17: W F7-F8 had 3-4 libs -> G7, G8, F9 captured it and saved my top).

## White vs 3-4 + framework that WON (G16, W+4 vs 16k)
B G6, W C4, B D7, W E5 (center between black's stones), B F5 attach, W F4 hane under,
B G4, W F3 solid (F4 had 2 libs), B D5, W E4 (E5 at 2 libs), B D4 wedge, W D3 (under the
wedge; C3/E3 cuts both fail), B E6, W C5 atari -> B D6. Then boundary moves in order:
G3, C6, B7 (hane, then B6 connect), H3, J2, H2, A6, C3. Area = rows 1-3 + left A-C to row 6
+ E4/E5/F4 = 39. KEY: every time a stone of mine had 2 libs, I connected it immediately,
and every wall stone was attached to my own territory (no sandwich). 39 is a thin win:
any lost edge point = draw, so keep doing solid fixes.

## Lesson T1 (G14): AS WHITE vs TENGEN, ROWS 6-9 ALONE = 36 = LOSS. NEED A LIVING 2ND AREA
- After W C4, B E3 (approach under), W G6, B G4: my D6 was on the wrong side of E5; B E6/D5/C5/
  C3 sealed C4. Better 3rd move: strengthen C4 toward its own side (C6 extension or D3/C3
  base) so the left side (A-C cols) stays mine, OR take the top-left corner low (C7) and treat
  C4 as aji. A white group needs ~10 pts outside the top to reach 38.
- Don't play gote 2-lib stones (B5 next to B C5 -> needed B6 at once). Play the solid point.
- Row-6 edge points (H6/J6) are worth 2-4 pts each in close games — take them before black.

## White vs tengen that worked (G12, draw vs 16k)
W C4, B C5 attach, W G6 (other side, high), B G5 attach, W D7 (top framework), B D4, W C3,
B D3, W D2/C2 (corner base), then W E6! (head of E5, links D7/G6), F6 & G7 solid fills,
H7/J7 block the right. Result: top rows 6-9 (~27) + small live corner (10) = 37 = draw.
To WIN by a few points: (1) after B B6 vs my C6, secure the top-left corner (B7/B8) before
black's B8 + A7-A6 crawl connects out (cost ~6 pts); (2) avoid making the A9-type point black's
sente (B9 atari left B9-C9 with 2 libs); (3) spend fewer moves on the corner if possible.

## Lesson H1 (G13): WHEN THE SPLIT ALREADY WINS, PLAY SOLID — COUNT FIRST
As Black, rows 1-5 = 45 = win. After C4, F4, E5 vs W C6, F6, E6 I owned row 5 if I just
played solid boundary moves (F5/G5 then D5, B5). I hane'd G6 (touching F6, 3 libs) and W
crosscut G5 -> I lost G5/H5/G6. RULE: before any hane into the opponent's side, count. If my
count is >= 45 already, play the solid move on my side of the boundary (e.g. G5 not G6).
A hane is only OK if the cut behind it (here G5/F5) is already covered.

## Lesson F1 (G11): AS BLACK, DON'T ACCEPT A 4-ROW vs 5-ROW SPLIT
- Rows 1-4 = 36 for White means I need ALL of rows 5-9 with zero White life there. Any
  surviving White group (8 pts) or one lost edge stone loses the game. When W attaches (D4 on
  my C4), keep a foothold on White's side: hane underneath (D3) or take the 3-3 (C3) so White's
  strip is split; don't spend a gote move (C5) defending a cut while W extends F4.
- To kill an invader on my side, CUT IT FROM ITS HOME FIRST: in G11 the W left group's only
  link was A4->B4; B4 (not B5) would have killed it.
- A hane stone left at 2 libs WILL be ataried next move (H4 after W H3 -> W H5). Connect it.

## Lesson E2 (G10): AS WHITE, KEEP A CORNER UNDER EVERY GROUP; EDGE-STRIP EYES
- vs B F6 I played D4 then D6 (same line) -> B C5 wedge, C4, D3 cut me off from BOTH left
  corners: my 7 stones were a wall with black on 3 sides. Next time as White: first move a
  3-3/3-4 corner point (C3, C7, G3...), second move the OTHER low corner. Answer a wedge on
  the side where the corner is (C4/C3 block the corner), so the group keeps an edge base.
- A group along the edge (H5-H9 with J6-J9 empty) whose end touches a LIVING enemy stone (J5)
  has only straight-three of real space -> dead to the vital point. Count eye space as points
  the opponent can't push into; secure the end (H4/J5) BEFORE it's taken.

## Lesson E1 (G9): COUNT MY OWN GROUP'S EYES BEFORE ATTACKING
My "territory" (rows 5-9, cols E-J) was enclosed by W stones on ALL sides once W played J5, H6,
J7 (right edge) and E8/F9/G8/H9 (top edge). Only F6 was an eye -> 20 stones dead.
=> An area isn't territory until its open edges are blocked; a stone on J5/J6 or row 9 by the
   opponent turns "territory" into a group needing eyes. Block edge crawls SOLIDLY (J6 had 2
   libs and was captured).
=> Before a capturing race, run libs.py and classify: whose group has an eye? Eye beats no-eye
   on shared liberties. Don't extend a reduction (G2/F2/G1/J2) into a race unless my count of
   private libs beats theirs (eye counts 2 for a 2-point eye).

## Lesson W1 (G8): AS WHITE, A CLEAN SPLIT LOSES — CONTEST THE BIG SIDE EARLY
White needs 38 of 81. A 4-row strip (rows 1-4 = 36) minus Black's edge stones is ~34 = loss.
Black opened G6 + D7 (top framework) and I answered low twice (C4, F4) and then followed every
Black move (B4, D4, E5, F5, G4). By move 20 Black's top (rows 5-9) was sealed.
=> By move 4-8 put a stone INTO the side Black is framing while it is still thin (e.g. vs G6/D7:
   W E7/F7 or a 3-3 at G8/C8), even if it gets attacked; a living group there is worth 15+.
=> Invading behind a finished wall (G8 at move 25) dies: Black's 1-2 point placement (H9) plus
   the 2nd-line hane (J6) leave one eye. If I must invade late, pick the widest edge (5+ points
   of 1st/2nd line) and take the 2-2/2nd-line base first.
=> Edge hane by Black on my side (A4): block solidly next to my wall; a lone atari stone (A3)
   got cut off and the whole corner (A1-B3) became Black's.

## Lesson 000 (G6): NO SANDWICHED WALLS
A wall with enemy stones on BOTH sides has no eye space and scores nothing. If the opponent
attaches on top of my stone (B C5 on W C4), do NOT extend along the same line again and again
(D4,E4,F4...). Either hane once and then switch to another corner, or secure the space below
the wall IMMEDIATELY (2nd/3rd-line move) before they invade it.
As White vs tengen E5: take two separate low corners (C3/C4 and G6/G7 side), make each a base
that lives on its own, then reduce Black's center. Score target 38 = two good corners/sides.

## Key facts
- Komi 7, area scoring, 81 points. Black WINS with >= 45 (44 = draw). White wins with >= 38
  (37 = draw).
- Area scoring: playing inside the opponent's territory late costs nothing, so probing
  invasions at the end are free (but they rarely work vs correct answers).
- Opponent passes when the game is settled; engine removes dead stones itself.
- The opponent (even 10k) answers ataris and connects cutting points correctly. It does make
  odd moves (G3: W F6 self-dead stone, W E1 filling own eye) but not game-losing ones.

## THE BIG RECURRING PROBLEM (all three games)
I end with ~31-38 points: center stones + a little. The opponent takes the edges.
A center wall does NOT score on 9x9. In G3 tengen E5 as Black gave White three sides
(left B5/C7, bottom G3-invasion, then the entire right edge by crawling down the J column).
=> Fixes to try next:
   a) Open in/near a corner-side point (C3/C4/G3/G7 style 3-4 points), not E5.
   b) When the opponent invades under my framework (e.g. W G3 under E5), DON'T cap from above
      (G4) — that hands them the 2nd line + edge. Block their base on the side where my
      territory is: e.g. play the hane/attach on the edge side (H3/F3 or J4-direction) so the
      invader lives small in one corner at most.
   c) Never let a single white stone crawl along MY edge (J4-J5-J6-J7-J8 in G3). Block in front
      of it early with a stone that has 3+ liberties.
   d) Count only points actually enclosed. In G3 at move ~40 I thought I had 42, true was 38.

## Black vs White tengen: WON TWICE (G15 B+10, G19 B+24) — REUSE THIS
G19 line: B C4, W E5, B G6, W C7, B F4, W E4, B E3, W F5, B G5, W D4, B D3 (diagonal link
to C4; W's C3/F3 cuts then fail), W C5, B C3, W G3 invasion -> B G4 (connect), H3 (block toward my side),
D2/G2/D1 kill. Then attack W's center group: it touches my walls on 3 sides, so run libs.py
on WHITE groups each move and cut them from their top friends while taking libs.

## Black vs White tengen that WON B+10 (G15)
B C4, W E5, B G6 (opposite side, 4th line), W C6, B F4 -> my area = bottom rows 1-4 + right
cols G-J. When W wedges (E4/D3), hane under (E3), hane at the head (G5), and fix with the
SOLID connection (F3) before anything else (an empty F3 was a double-atari point). Then
block each side on its edge: C3/C2 (left), G7/G8 (top right; G8 cut W's H8 probe off F8).
From move ~30 count every move: 45 needed; with 48+ just play solid (B5 not A6).

## Opening that WON (G5, as Black): C4, then F4 (other low corner/4th line), then E5.
Board splits rows 1-5 (45 = Black win) vs rows 6-9.
G7 variant: B C4, W E3 (low), B F4, W C3 attach -> B D4, E4 (solid), hane B3/H3 and connect
B4/H4 when the hane stone has 2 libs. White's bottom = ~22 pts, I own rows 4-9 IF I stop the
top invasion. Lesson G7: a 5-row-high open top lets White live on the edge. Kill an invader by
taking its EDGE/BASE side (block on row 8/9 or the 3-3), not by blocking from below it each
time (D6/F7/G7/B7 pushed it along row 8 and it made eyes C9/E9). Ideally pre-empt with a
3rd-line stone (D7 or F7), not E7. Then hold row 5 (D5, C5, F5) and kill any
invasion under it: cut it from its outside friend first (G4 between G3 and G6), block crawls.

## Lessons
